---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  COMMENT_REACTION_BOOST,
  FORMULA_TEXT,
  FORMULA_VERSION,
  NORMALIZATION_REFERENCES,
  QUALIFICATION_VERSION,
  SCORE_WEIGHTS,
  WORKAROUND_WEIGHTS,
} from '../lib/signals/constants';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Methodology — Developer Signals"
  description="Versioned qualification and scoring methodology for the Developer Signals report."
  current="methodology"
>
  <section class="hero">
    <p class="hero__eyebrow">Formula {FORMULA_VERSION}</p>
    <h1>Methodology</h1>
    <p class="hero__lede">
      This report is a reproducible prioritization signal, not a browser roadmap.
      Raw evidence remains visible and linked so readers can disagree with the model.
    </p>
  </section>

  <article class="prose">
    <h2>Composite priority</h2>
    <p class="formula"><code>{FORMULA_TEXT}</code></p>
    <p>
      Scores are published on a 0–100 scale. The weights are
      {` ${SCORE_WEIGHTS.votes * 100}% votes, ${SCORE_WEIGHTS.useCases * 100}% use-case evidence, and ${SCORE_WEIGHTS.workarounds * 100}% workaround burden.`}
      Issue age is never a positive or negative input.
    </p>

    <h2>Normalization</h2>
    <p>
      Each raw metric uses <code>min(1, log1p(value) / log1p(reference))</code>.
      Version {FORMULA_VERSION} reference values are
      {` ${NORMALIZATION_REFERENCES.votes} votes, ${NORMALIZATION_REFERENCES.useCases} effective qualified authors, and ${NORMALIZATION_REFERENCES.workaroundBurden} weighted workaround points.`}
      The logarithm preserves differences at low evidence levels without letting
      a viral outlier flatten the rest of the report.
    </p>

    <h2>Qualified use cases</h2>
    <p>
      Qualification rules version {QUALIFICATION_VERSION} parse the repository’s
      two headings, require a concrete developer goal or scenario, and require
      enough original content to convey the need.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>Bots, minimized content, moderation, and thread-management messages are excluded.</li>
      <li>Template-only, generic support, and standards-debate-only comments are excluded.</li>
      <li>Authors are deduplicated per feature; only their strongest qualifying contribution counts.</li>
      <li>
        Positive reactions on qualifying comments add
        {` ${COMMENT_REACTION_BOOST.perReaction} evidence points each, capped at ${COMMENT_REACTION_BOOST.maximumPerAuthor} per author and ${COMMENT_REACTION_BOOST.maximumShareOfQualifiedAuthors * 100}% of the author count.`}
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Workaround burden</h2>
    <p>
      Deterministic phrases classify each qualified author’s strongest evidence.
      Fixed weights are:
    </p>
    <ul>
      {Object.entries(WORKAROUND_WEIGHTS).map(([severity, weight]) => (
        <li><strong>{severity}</strong>: {weight}</li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p>
      Categories cover minor fallbacks, polyfills or custom implementations,
      duplicated systems or excess payload, and blocked scenarios with no viable fallback.
    </p>

    <h2>Confidence and trends</h2>
    <p>
      Confidence summarizes evidence volume, vote breadth, and the share of
      reviewed comments that qualify. It is displayed but never added to priority.
      Daily snapshots retain raw metrics and their formula version; the interface
      shows seven- and thirty-day changes without making momentum the default sort.
    </p>

    <h2>Overrides and optional AI</h2>
    <p>
      Version-controlled manual overrides run last and must include a rationale,
      reviewer, and date. Optional AI enrichment is disabled by default, schema
      constrained, and limited to classification, themes, and clearly labeled
      summaries. It cannot assign an opaque numeric quality score or replace the
      linked source comment.
    </p>

    <h2>Change governance</h2>
    <p>
      Weight, reference, or qualification changes require a version increment,
      regenerated snapshots, sensitivity tests, and a documented change entry.
      The initial weights are hypotheses; feedback belongs in the project issue tracker.
    </p>
    <h3>History</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>1.0.0 — 2026-09-08:</strong> Initial MVP formula and qualification rules.</li>
    </ul>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
